e":"For the Record - The 70s","n":2}],"lists":[],"n_lists":0}}],"members":[],"member_of":[],"genres":[{"id":30277598324326,"type":"genre","name":"pop","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1523,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_genres":[{"id":247802226836647,"type":"genre","name":"Brill Building","n":4},{"id":179554264188962,"type":"genre","name":"Countrypolitan","n":1},{"id":169140360411371,"type":"genre","name":"Lounge","n":1},{"id":56173113450769,"type":"genre","name":"Swing","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":169050477627223,"name":"Antes del diluvio","book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","start":1957,"end":1962,"desc":"La «edad oscura» del rock'n'roll: los rebeldes son sustituidos por ídolos adolescentes educados y el Brill Building devuelve el poder a los compositores profesionales. Pero en esos años se incuban la revolución futura: la guitarra fuzz de Link Wray, los bailes de moda (el twist), el rock instrumental de guitarras (Duane Eddy, Ventures, Shadows), el calipso y el primer ska, el doo-wop más inventivo (Coasters, Drifters, Five Royales), el nacimiento del soul (Sam Cooke, Ray Charles, Motown, Stax), el estudio como instrumento de Joe Meek y el surf de los Beach Boys y del «muro de sonido» de Phil Spector.","color":"#f59e0b","family":"Raíces: rock'n'roll, country y roots","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#before-flood"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":167764867783506,"artist":"Paul Anka","artist_id":137926844206700,"title":"Diana","year":1957,"ess":0,"yt":"98Q0fuBwt-g","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Antes del diluvio","scene_id":169050477627223,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#before-flood","chapters":["Sección 2.3"],"pages":[[1,17,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":17,"text":"…lascivious, insolent rocker was quickly replaced by a generation of polite, well-dressed, romantic \"teen-idols\" that ruled the airwaves till 1965. Paul Anka's Diana (1957) and Pat Boone's Love Letters In The Sand (1957) were emblematic. At best, rock'n'roll was fused with country music to yield a more…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Pre-1970 Exodus to US","body":"Paul Anka, a promising Canadian artist before 1970, moved to the United States because the nascent Canadian music industry offered limited opportunities to make a living. \"Neil Young, Jody Mitchell, Paul Anke, Leonard Cohen, all of our biggest and most promising artists beat a path for the United States.\"","year":1970,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The 90s Part 8: The CanRock Revolution","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U2kYleeMnl8"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Backlash for \"Having My Baby\"","body":"Paul Anka faced significant backlash at the time of \"Having My Baby\"'s release in 1974, particularly from the women's liberation movement. Critics objected to the lyrics, which they felt implied male ownership (using \"my baby\" instead of \"our baby\") and presented a woman's pregnancy as a demonstration of her love for a man. Anka himself wrote the song when his first wife, Ann, was pregnant and remained unapologetic about the controversy.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 48 - The Worst of the 70s The Music of 1974","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 48 - The Worst of the 70s The Music of 1974","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Early Teen Idol Success","body":"Paul Anka achieved teen idol status in the 1950s, notably securing a hit with \"Diana\" when he was just 16 years old.","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 48 - The Worst of the 70s The Music of 1974","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 48 - The Worst of the 70s The Music of 1974","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Flow to America","body":"Paul Anka was among the ambitious and talented Canadian musicians who flowed south to seek their fortune in America. This was a common trend when domestic acceptance and popularity for Canadian artists often only came after achieving success in the US. \"ambitious, talented Canadian musicians flowed south to seek their fortune in America.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Roots of the 90s CanRock Explosion","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6PGgNCaW4xo"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Frank Sinatra Collaboration","body":"Paul Anka enjoyed a unique relationship with Frank Sinatra, who would occasionally ask him to write songs. One notable result of this collaboration was the song \"My Way.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 48 - The Worst of the 70s The Music of 1974","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 48 - The Worst of the 70s The Music of 1974","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Juno Award for Album of the Year (1975)","body":"Nominated.","year":1975,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6314042"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Juno Award for Single of the Year (1975)","body":"Nominated.","year":1975,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6314103"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Juno Award for Songwriter of the Year (1975)","body":"Nominated.","year":1975,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6314105"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Juno Award for Album of the Year (1976)","body":"Nominated.","year":1976,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://ww